Title: Multimodal cheminformatic and network biology analysis enables deeper mechanistic understanding of PFAS-induced hepatotoxicity.
Abstract: Per- and polyfluoroalkyl substances (PFAS) are persistent and ubiquitous environmental contaminants associated with diverse adverse health outcomes. Despite prior extensive molecular analyses, mechanistic understanding of PFAS hepatotoxicity remains incomplete. In recent years, human liver spheroids have emerged as a useful tool in this space for investigating human-relevant responses to PFAS exposure across longer-term exposure periods. Here, we reprocessed publicly available PFAS RNA-sequencing data from human liver spheroids using the R-ODAF pipeline to generate a high-confidence set of differentially expressed genes (DEGs) and applied an integrated multimodal platform combining cheminformatic target prediction and transcription factor (TF) activity inference within a liver-specific prior knowledge network. While conventional pathway enrichment recapitulated known PFAS effects on SREBF- and PPAR-driven lipid metabolism, it provided limited insight for compounds with fewer DEGs, such as PFBS. Multimodal integration revealed compound-specific regulatory programs, with PFOS and PFOA exhibiting a time- and dose-dependent suppression of a conserved TF module comprising SREBF, CEBP, and HNF family members. Cheminformatic protein target predictions for the various PFAS compounds highlighted several common targets, including MAP3K5 and PLCG1, as well as several compound-specific target predictions. Incorporating these predictions with network biology analyses elucidated upstream cascades differentiating PFOA- versus PFOS-mediated regulation of lipid metabolism, with the PFOA-specific inhibition of PTPN7 being a discriminating feature, highlighting potential molecular bases for compound-specific toxicological profiles. Collectively, our findings demonstrate that integrating structural, transcriptomic, and network-biology approaches enhances mechanistic resolution beyond traditional enrichment analyses, providing testable hypotheses for PFAS-induced perturbations and guiding experimental validation of key targets.
Journal: BMC bioinformatics

Title: Combined exposure to PFOA and its substitute hexafluoropropylene oxide dimer acid (HFPO-DA) elicits distinct lipid metabolic abnormalities from single exposures.
Abstract: After perfluorooctanoic acid (PFOA) was restricted, hexafluoropropylene oxide dimer acid (HFPO-DA) was introduced as a substitute, but its hepatic safety has not been adequately validated. In this study, physiologically based pharmacokinetic (PBPK) modeling predicted that HFPO-DA and PFOA accumulate in tissue. Then, we compared thelipotoxicity of PFOA, HFPO-DA, and their binary combination in mouse primary hepatocytes. The results showed that both single compounds induced lipid droplet accumulation. HFPO-DA elicited more extensive lipidomic perturbations. HFPO-DA exposure is associated with enhanced de novo lipogenesis, diacylglycerol accumulation, suppressed transcription of core insulin signaling genes (Ins2, Irs1, Irs2, Akt2, Pik3ca), and upregulated ceramide synthase genes (Cers4 and Cers6). These effects may reflect PPARα-mediated effects. These transcriptional changes suggest a potential disruption in insulin signaling, and the upregulation of ceramide synthase genes raises the possibility that ceramide accumulation may contribute to impaired Akt signaling. In terms of immune responses, PFOA preferentially activated the NF-κB/Th17 inflammatory combined-exposure group, in contrast to HFPO-DA, whereas HFPO-DA primarily acted through ceramide accumulation and Fcγ receptor-mediated phagocytosis. More critically, HFPO-DA significantly suppressed cardiolipin synthesis-related genes (crls1, taz, pgs1), impaired mitochondrial biogenesis, and exacerbated oxidative stress. Strikingly, combined exposure produced effects that were not simply additive. Lipidomic profiling revealed downregulation of glycerolipid intermediates (MG, DG, and TG) in the combined exposure group, contrasting with the accumulation observed under single exposures. This shift was accompanied by activation of the necroptosis pathway and upregulation of Caspase-3, Caspase-9, and PPARα/γ, suggesting that co-exposure pushes the liver from a lipid-storage state toward oxidation-dominated metabolism. Moreover, combined exposure triggers additional toxicological cascades beyond those elicited by either compound alone, raising serious concerns about the safety of HFPO-DA as a replacement chemical, particularly in the context of combined human exposure to multiple PFAS.
Journal: Environmental pollution (Barking, Essex : 1987)

Title: Decoding cellular vulnerabilities for interpretable drug response prediction.
Abstract: Accurate prediction of anticancer drug response remains challenging because conventional approaches based on chemical structures and baseline molecular profiles do not directly capture drug-induced perturbations or cellular dependencies. Here, we present vulnerability-guided cascaded attention transformer (VCAT), which integrates Connectivity Map (CMap)/Library of Integrated Network-based Cellular Signatures (LINCS)-derived consensus transcriptional signatures with clustered regularly interspaced short palindromic repeats (CRISPR)-supervised cellular representations through adaptive encoders and dual stream cascaded attention. Across four data splitting strategies, VCAT outperformed conventional machine learning and published deep learning models, achieving the best performance in 11 of 12 classification split metric comparisons. Ablation analyses showed that CRISPR-derived vulnerability features complement baseline gene expression and that the proposed interaction architecture further improves prediction. VCAT also enhanced continuous IC50 prediction and generalized across Cancer Therapeutics Response Portal (CTRP), Profiling Relative Inhibition Simultaneously in Mixtures (PRISM), patient-derived organoids, and hepatocellular carcinoma cohorts. Attention based prioritization and integrated gradients jointly highlighted biologically relevant genes, while knockdown of cytosolic iron-sulfur assembly component 3 (CIAO3), methylenetetrahydrofolate dehydrogenase 2 (MTHFD2), and WD repeat domain 1 (WDR1) increased sorafenib sensitivity in hepatocellular carcinoma cell lines. These findings support integrating drug-induced perturbations with genetic vulnerabilities for accurate, interpretable, and transferable anticancer drug response prediction.
Journal: Briefings in bioinformatics

Title: SHERLOCK: Structured representation learning and causal inference of downstream perturbation effects.
Abstract: Understanding the effects of genetic, molecular, and experimental perturbations is essential for decoding cellular mechanisms and guiding biomedical interventions. Existing computational approaches are typically designed for individual tasks, such as predicting perturbation responses, characterizing downstream transcriptional effects, or modeling perturbation combinations, and therefore do not provide a unified framework for learning interpretable perturbation representations while enabling causal analysis of downstream effects and characterization of condition-dependent responses. We present SHERLOCK, an interpretable deep generative framework for single-cell perturbation analysis that represents perturbation effects as structured interventions on a latent baseline cellular state. SHERLOCK learns correlated and sparse perturbation representations that organize genetic and pharmacological perturbations according to shared transcriptional responses. % By formulating perturbations as interventions within a structural causal model, it enables counterfactual estimation of their downstream transcriptional effects under explicit identifiability assumptions. The same framework quantifies how perturbation responses vary across conditions and compositionally models combinatorial perturbations, enabling prediction of held-out combinations and classification of genetic interactions. Across genome-scale CRISPR, chemical, and spatial perturbation datasets, SHERLOCK recovers perturbation relationships concordant with known biological pathways and pharmacological properties, identifies condition-dependent responses, and predicts combinatorial perturbation effects. Together, SHERLOCK provides a unified framework for interpretable and causal analysis of perturbation effects across diverse single-cell perturbation experiments.
Journal: bioRxiv : the preprint server for biology

Title: Mycosubtilin suppresses HepG2 proliferation, binds p38α in vitro, and engages ER stress-associated MAPK signaling.
Abstract: Hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC) is a highly lethal malignancy with limited durable responses to current therapies. This study evaluated the effects of mycosubtilin (Myco), a Bacillus subtilis-derived cyclic lipopeptide, on HepG2 cell proliferation and xenograft tumor growth, and investigated its anti-HCC mechanisms using temporal transcriptomic profiling, molecular simulations, and surface plasmon resonance (SPR). Myco significantly suppressed HepG2 proliferation in a dose- and time-dependent manner and inhibited xenograft tumor growth, with preliminary tolerability indicated by stable body weight and histopathological examination of selected organs. The antiproliferative effect of Myco was further confirmed in Huh7 human hepatocellular carcinoma cells, with IC
Journal: Bioorganic chemistry
